notes:
Trick to remember OSI model layers - Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away.
Packet - Piece of data from layer 3 (Network) of OSI model. Contains info such as an IP header and payload.
  Headers:
    Time to live - Timer for the packet to not clog up your network if it never manages to reach a host or escape.
    Checksum - Provides integrity checking for protocols such as TCP/IP. If any data is changed, this value will be different from what was expected therefore corrupt.
    Source address - IP address of the device that packet is being sent from so that data knows where to return to.
    Destination address - The IP address the package is being sent to so that data knows where to travel next.
Frame - Piece of data used at layer 2 (Data)of the OSI model. Encapsulates the packet and adds additional info such as MAC addresses.
TCP (Transmission control protocol) - Packets sent with precision.
  Three-way handshake - The term given for the process used to establish a connection between two devices.
    Step 1 SYN - A SYN message is the initial packet sent by a client during the handshake. Used to initiate a conection and sync the wto devices together.
    Step 2 SYN/ACK - Packet sent by the receiving device (server) to acknowledge the sync attempt from the client.
    Step 3 ACK - The acknowledgement packet can be used by either client or server to acknowledge that a series of mesages/packets have been successfully received.
    Step 4 DATA - Once a connection has been established, data such as bytes of a file is sent via the "Data" message.
    Step 5 FIN - Used to cleanly (properly) close the connection after it has been complete.



